 a guy who was a he was a  canadian law student then he became a  a lawyer in new york he went to harvard  law school  worked in new york and he uh he was here  on a work visa  and he wanted to  um  according to his complaint that he filed  he wanted to go to kinko's uh and  make flyers spend 50 cents making flyers  saying  re-elect president obama and he wanted  to hand those flyers out in central park  well because he was not a citizen  uh even though he's in the the united  states and has other kinds of first  member rights even though he's not  assistant he would violate federal law  he could go to jail for years face ten  thousand dollar fine if he hands that  piece of paper to one person because  then he'd be engaging in election  related activities a non-citizen  and this case went up and the supreme  court had a chance to explain why  as it said it says united if the  identity of the speaker doesn't matter  and the first amendment really is always  about more speech all the time in  elections why is it that this guy  benjamin blumen could be shut up and the  supreme court  didn't even hear the case  it came up on a special kind of appeal  from a three-judge court  which means that when the court declined  to hear the case it agreed  that the lower court was right that this  law is constitutional so we now have  this new anomaly which is that  we say that non-human entities  these corporations that only exist  because the state allows you to create  this this fake thing  non-human entities have these first  amendment rights but yet a human being  who is living in new york who wants to  spend 50 cents he couldn't do that and  so what's the difference uh if it's  really true that the identity of the  speaker doesn't matter which is what the  supreme court said is citizens united  well certainly the identity of the  speaker matters when that speaker is a  foreign individual or a foreign  government or a foreign corporation  so the it seems like the majority  opinion they're not saying that large  corporations being able to funnel tens  or hundreds of millions of dollars  for or against a campaign independently  of the the candidate that that won't  necessarily corrupt but it's more of  by trying to regulate it in the way that  the mccain feingold act does it actually  has far-reaching consequences uh or  trying to regulate it in the way that  the federal election commission was  trying to regulate it with citizens  united it has far-reaching consequences  that touch on just general free speech  issues like what you just talked about a  you know a sentence in a book that  happens to be published or sold during  the election or a movie made by a random  filmmaker i think for one thing for  example a lot of people again they they  say they resent this idea corporations  aren't people but they don't realize  corporations have always you know again  had some of these rights i acted as  corporations and uh and and acted in the  political arena in a variety of ways i  think what these people should ask  themselves is well if exxon were not  incorporated  would they go oh  well exxon's not incorporated well  that's fine let them spend all the money  they want right and and conversely i  find most of these people if you say  well should we be able to censor  the new york times it's a corporation  they say well no no no  not that and and and when you see that i  think it makes people realize the the  question is not really whether it's a  corporation speaking or not the question  is  whether or not we think the first  amendment protects the expenditure  of money we actually had to have rules  before citizens united to differentiate  because we actually this was the law  that  um google and general motors could not  spend their funds directly on election  speech but  the new york times and fox news could  and so we had tests and the federal  election commission had tests and there  are just some disagreements about how to  best do it i would point to the work of  professor sonja west at uh the  university of georgia has written  extensively on this and she said the  test should be people who are regularly  engaged in journalism that doesn't mean  it has to be objective and you can't  have a point of view it doesn't mean  there's a problem with fox news or msnbc  because they might have a point of view  but it means you're doing some something  other than electioneering i do think  that there's some sense yes that's what  i'm saying that these are these are some  of the cost of having a vibrant robust  first amendment and if you uh want to  give the government the power to  regulate you know books and movies you  should think about who is the politician  you most hate is it hillary clinton is  it donald trump is it you know somebody  else do you want them in charge of  deciding who gets to speak out in  politics and in what way and in what  fashion in what manner uh and and i  think you should think about it in those  terms and that might that sometimes  makes people realize may  be it's best to let things  go now i i would say however having said  that that a lot of the arguments about  corporate influence are are vastly  overblown there's a quite a bit of  debate really about whether or not  corporations in fact benefit uh from  spending money on politics uh  corporations big corporations the really  you know the fortune 500 types what they  really like to do is spend money on  lobbying uh that's much more direct uh  they can talk to candidates directly  they can let other people elect folks  there's almost certain to be some folks  elected who they will like and they can  go work with those people and craft  messages and bills and they can do this  more behind the scenes and that way they  don't have to offend any of their  customers who who don't like the party  that they're supporting but it's also  clear that that just spending money  doesn't automatically win elections uh  you can look at the last presidential  election i mean donald trump's been  about all told about a quarter of what  the hillary clinton forces spent it  certainly helps to have money uh but in  the end uh it's not dispositive what  what the monies does is it's spent to  try to inform voters and those voters go  out and vote at the polls  so we've learned that in citizens united  the supreme court ruled that political  spending by corporations associations  and labor unions is a form of protected  speech under the first amendment but  whether or how the government can limit  free speech and campaign contributions  is still a matter of debate  as rick hayson notes one test for the  difference between the free speech  rights accorded to journalists and those  of corporations might be whether they  are entities regularly engaged in  journalism but as bradley smith points  out allowing the government to impose  limits on how people or corporations  speak out in politics may lead to  unintended consequences  to learn more about citizens united  visit the national constitution center's  interactive constitution and khan  academy's resources on u.s government  and politics

